<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=runes">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Runes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as runic alphabets, futharks, rune-rows</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Runes are the letters of a set of related alphabets, known as rune-rows or futharks, native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>. The earliest secure runic inscriptions date from at the latest 150 CE, and the oldest clear inscriptions are found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The script ultimately stems from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>, though the exact development is unclear. The Elder Futhark, of 24 runes, was used c. 150 to 800 CE, with the first complete ordering on the Kylver Stone c. 400. Runes were replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet with Christianisation, by about 700 in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and 1100 in northern Europe, but persisted for special purposes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/runes.jpg" alt="Runes" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bengt Olof ÅRADSSON · CC BY 1.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rökstenen_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of wisdom, poetry, magic, war and the dead: he gave an eye to drink from the well of wisdom and hanged himself for nine nights on the world tree to win the knowledge of the <b>runes</b>, and his two ravens flew across the world to bring him news.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Old English was first written in <b>runes</b>, using the futhorc, a set derived from the 24-character elder futhark and extended by five more runes for Anglo-Saxon vowel sounds.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>The Younger Futhark, with only sixteen <b>runes</b>, used one sign for several sounds, so that runic inscriptions can be hard to interpret.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>The memorial stone of the Turkic prince Kul Tigin, raised in 732, carries an inscription in Old Turkic <b>runes</b> on three sides and, on the fourth, a Chinese text composed by the Tang emperor Xuanzong.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vikings spoke Old Norse and wrote it in <b>runes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by c. 150 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Denmark and northern Germany</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A possible earlier inscription, the Meldorf fibula, dates to around 50 CE, and Tacitus's Germania, from around 98 CE, may describe the use of rune-like inscriptions. Raetic, Venetic, Etruscan and Old Latin scripts have all been proposed as the immediate source.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet<span>c. 1050 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=runes">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
